ry out performances  consistently so when we were thrown into  situations that are not you know the  same as other situations that we've seen  so we're a little bit unprepared for  them they the particulars are different  Aristotle would say we can carry out  what an education we call transfer of  knowledge right so if I understand what  it means to be generous in this context  I can then if I really have virtue  probably transfer that over here to this  context I might get mixed up about some  of the particulars but I can get  straightened out about them and then I  can see how to make that work and if  it's similar with the crafts that we  have if you really are a good carpenter  it's not just doing the same thing over  and over and over again you can adapt to  new situations and see you know looking  through them how do we do this great  example of this my uncle master  craftsman now in two different fields he  was a drywaller  since high school and at first drywall  is very rough stuff and hanging it  there's not an awful lot of art involved  although there's some because I mean  just go into the house where somebody's  done bad drywall and you can see the  effects of it right it's one of those  sort of things where the less you can  notice it the better it is and that's  not just due to the hanger it's also due  to the mutter and and other things  however as his career progressed because  he was an intelligent guy and interested  in what he was doing and picking up  things along the way from older people  there is sort of apprenticeship type of  type of  activity he learned how to do amazing  stuff with drywall where he could you  know wet it and then bend it the right  way and he could walk into a place and  say we're gonna make this many sheets  for this and I'm gonna have to cut it  this way for that weird little bed up  there  that is a prime example of craft  knowledge just as much as a musician who  has to figure out how to adapt a  Prokofiev piece to a different ensemble  of instruments and it's something very  admirable  now the virtues are like that in many  respects they provide us with the  ability to produce consistent  performance not so much in in terms of  production but now in terms of our  actions and our emotions so there are  analogies between the virtues and the  tec-9 that's the plural for tech name  the crafts one of these is that you're  only a real craft person if you actually  possess the knowledge of the correct if  you're just doing things by going on the  internet and watching a few videos and  then seeing kind of do it you're not  anything they could be part of your  training  but you're not yet a craftsperson if in  music you know you're still using the  tablature you kind of really learn the  song yet at that point having and so on  so - you could only really say that  somebody has the knowledge that goes  with a craft if they really do in fact  exhibit that if they're going by  somebody else's guidance they're not yet  at that point we can say the same thing  about virtue can't wait if you always  have to be told what the right thing to  do is you are not yet virtuous you may  in fact be doing virtuous actions if you  have a question about the distinction  between doing virtuous actions and being  virtuous go and take a look at the  about virgin as a norm for moral  evaluation there is a very important  difference there a kid can be a good kid  because there still within the framework  of punishment and reward and they do the  right thing but they're not yet virtuous  they are only able to do that when  they're truly on their own and can  actuate that knowledge another very very  important likening between crafts people  and those who are virtuous is this  entire notion of the mean Aristotle  thinks that it's not just in moral  issues but also in the craft that we're  trying to find the mean or the middle  position because that is what is best he  gives the example of works of art where  yo